openapi.json in this directory is a REAL `zigbase openapi` document, not a
hand-written stand-in. It is what `zigapagos migrate --from rails --backend`
reads to learn which operations a Rails route may be bound to, so a fixture
that invented its own bytes would test the migrator against a document
shape ZigBase does not emit -- exactly the drift `src/cli/rails/backend.zig`
exists to survive.

It is checked in rather than generated at test time because the e2e must be
hermetic: it runs wherever CI runs, with no ZigBase binary on PATH.

REGENERATING IT (the documented developer step)

  zb=/path/to/zigbase                       # a built zigbase binary
  d=$(mktemp -d)

  # 1. Create the database. `schema dump`/`apply` need the system tables,
  #    and only `migrate` writes them.
  "$zb" migrate --data-dir "$d"

  # 2. Apply the two collections this fixture's Rails app maps onto:
  #    an AUTH collection `users` (its `email` required, createRule
  #    `@public` -- the sign-up form must reach it while signed out), and a
  #    BASE collection `posts` (`title` required text, `body` editor,
  #    listRule/viewRule `@public`, createRule `@request.auth.id != ""`).
  #    schema.json is beside this README.
  "$zb" schema apply "$(dirname "$0")/schema.json" --data-dir "$d"

  # 3. Export. --api-version is what lands in `info.version`, which is what
  #    the handoff reports as `backend.contract_version` (this document
  #    carries no `x-zigbase-contract-version`).
  "$zb" openapi --data-dir "$d" --api-version 1.0.0 --out openapi.json

WHAT THE E2E DEPENDS ON

  openapi: "3.1.2"                       backend.parse accepts 3.x only
  info.version: "1.0.0"                  -> handoff backend.contract_version
  10 operation ids                       listUsers createUsers viewUsers
                                         updateUsers deleteUsers listPosts
                                         createPosts viewPosts updatePosts
                                         deletePosts
  components.schemas.UsersCreate         carries BOTH `password` and
                                         `passwordConfirm`, which is the
                                         ONLY way an auth collection is
                                         recognisable -- it is what makes
                                         `users` the legal artifact for the
                                         RAILS_AUTH_JOURNEY answer
  x-zigbase-coverage.allAuthMethods      false, as always: auth-with-password
                                         and auth-logout are NOT in the
                                         document, which is why the journey's
                                         two session endpoints are the
                                         client's method names rather than
                                         operation ids

Do NOT reconcile this document with `fixture_document` in
src/cli/rails/backend.zig. They are independent by design: the inline one
gives `listPosts` a `conditional` access so every `x-zigbase-access`
spelling has a unit test, while a real `listRule: "@public"` (this one)
makes it `public`. Editing either to match the other costs a case its only
coverage.
